value}} (1–7). Higher scores describe a more outgoing, enthusiastic style; lower scores describe a more reserved, quiet style. This very brief two-item estimate cannot show the full range of how you behave across settings. This continuous self-description uses Holistic House explanatory prose, not a validated interpretation category; scale endpoints describe possible scores, not norms or percentiles. Mean of 2 responses, after reversing the items specified by the key. No verified clinical categories are assigned to this result here.","presentationDirection":"neutral","recommendedReview":false,"reliableDirection":true,"source":"https://gosling.psy.utexas.edu/scales-weve-developed/ten-item-personality-measure-tipi/ten-item-personality-inventory-tipi/","summary":"Higher scores describe a more outgoing, enthusiastic style; lower scores describe a more reserved, quiet style. This very brief two-item estimate cannot show the full range of how you behave across settings. This continuous self-description uses Holistic House explanatory prose, not a validated interpretation category; scale endpoints describe possible scores, not norms or percentiles.","verified":true},"values":[1,1.5,2,2.5,3,3.5,4,4.5,5,5.5,6,6.5,7]}]},{"allowedValues":[1,1.5,2,2.5,3,3.5,4,4.5,5,5.5,6,6.5,7],"mode":"descriptive","signature":{"dimensionClass":"trait","direction":"non-normative","key":"trait.tipi.agreeableness","max":7,"min":1,"sourceConstruct":"Agreeableness","timeframe":"general-self-description","unit":"points"},"source":"https://gosling.psy.utexas.edu/scales-weve-developed/ten-item-personality-measure-tipi/ten-item-personality-inventory-tipi/","variants":[{"template":{"actions":["For {{value}} (1–7) on agreeableness, record a situation that fits this description and an exception. Explore flexibility rather than aiming for a “correct” score.","If useful: In a disagreement, try expressing your position clearly while checking that you understood the other person''s view."],"bands":[],"basis":"descriptive","code":"continuous","construct":"Agreeableness","continuous":true,"currentBand":null,"label":"Descriptive score (1–7)","limitations":["No verified categorical cutoffs are applied to this version here. The score is not a diagnosis, norm or percentile."],"meaning":"Agreeableness: {{value}} (1–7). Higher scores describe more warmth and sympathy and less quarrelsomeness; lower scores describe more critical or confrontational self-descriptions. Two items cannot establish fairness, trustworthiness or relationship compatibility. This continuous self-description uses Holistic House explanatory prose, not a validated interpretation category; scale endpoints describe possible scores, not norms or percentiles. Mean of 2 responses, after reversing the items specified by the key. No verified clinical categories are assigned to this result here.","presentationDirection":"neutral","recommendedReview":false,"reliableDirection":true,"source":"https://gosling.psy.utexas.edu/scales-weve-developed/ten-item-personality-measure-tipi/ten-item-personality-inventory-tipi/","summary":"Higher scores describe more warmth and sympathy and less quarrelsomeness; lower scores describe more critical or confrontational self-descriptions. Two items cannot establish fairness, trustworthiness or relationship compatibility. This continuous self-description uses Holistic House explanatory prose, not a validated interpretation category; scale endpoints describe possible scores, not norms or percentiles.","verified":true},"values":[1,1.5,2,2.5,3,3.5,4,4.5,5,5.5,6,6.5,7]}]},{"allowedValues":[1,1.5,2,2.5,3,3.5,4,4.5,5,5.5,6,6.5,7],"mode":"descriptive","signature":{"dimensionClass":"trait","direction":"non-normative","key":"trait.tipi.conscientiousness","max":7,"min":1,"sourceConstruct":"Conscientiousness","timeframe":"general-self-description","unit":"points"},"source":"https://gosling.psy.utexas.edu/scales-weve-developed/ten-item-personality-measure-tipi/ten-item-personality-inventory-tipi/","variants":[{"template":{"actions":["For {{value}} (1–7) on conscientiousness, record a situation that fits this description and an exception. Explore flexibility rather than aiming for a “correct” score.","If useful: Choose one commitment and make its next step and timing explicit, adjusting it if the workload is unrealistic."],"bands":[],"basis":"descriptive","code":"continuous","construct":"Conscientiousness","continuous":true,"currentBand":null,"label":"Descriptive score (1–7)","limitations":["No verified categorical cutoffs are applied to this version here. The score is not a diagnosis, norm or percentile."],"meaning":"Conscientiousness: {{value}} (1–7). Higher scores describe more dependability, self-discipline and organization; lower scores describe more disorganization or carelessness in self-description. This broad two-item estimate does not explain why a particular commitment was hard to keep. This continuous self-description uses Holistic House explanatory prose, not a validated interpretation category; scale endpoints describe possible scores, not norms or percentiles. Mean of 2 responses, after reversing the items specified by the key. No verified clinical categories are assigned to this result here.","presentationDirection":"neutral","recommendedReview":false,"reliableDirection":true,"source":"https://gosling.psy.utexas.edu/scales-weve-developed/ten-item-personality-measure-tipi/ten-item-personality-inventory-tipi/","summary":"Higher scores describe more dependability, self-discipline and organization; lower scores describe more disorganization or carelessness in self-description. This broad two-item estimate does not explain why a particular commitment was hard to keep. This continuous self-description uses Holistic House explanatory prose, not a validated interpretation category; scale endpoints describe possible scores, not norms or percentiles.","verified":true},"values":[1,1.5,2,2.5,3,3.5,4,4.5,5,5.5,6,6.5,7]}]},{"allowedValues":[1,1.5,2,2.5,3,3.5,4,4.5,5,5.5,6,6.5,7],"mode":"descriptive","signature":{"dimensionClass":"trait","direction":"non-normative","key":"trait.tipi.emotional_stability","max":7,"min":1,"sourceConstruct":"Emotional stability","timeframe":"general-self-description","unit":"points"},"source":"https://gosling.psy.utexas.edu/scales-weve-developed/ten-item-personality-measure-tipi/ten-item-personality-inventory-tipi/","variants":[{"template":{"actions":["For {{value}} (1–7) on emotional stability, record a situation that fits this description and an exception. Explore flexibility rather than aiming for a “correct” score.","If useful: Recall a stressful moment and identify what supported calm or what support was missing, without treating steadiness as an obligation."],"bands":[],"basis":"descriptive","code":"continuous","construct":"Emotional stability","continuous":true,"currentBand":null,"label":"Descriptive score (1–7)","limitations":["No verified categorical cutoffs are applied to this version here. The score is not a diagnosis, norm or percentile."],"meaning":"Emotional stability: {{value}} (1–7). Higher scores describe more calm and emotional steadiness; lower scores describe more anxiety and becoming upset easily. The direction is opposite to a neuroticism score. Two items cannot diagnose anxiety or assess coping in every situation. This continuous self-description uses Holistic House explanatory prose, not a validated interpretation category; scale endpoints describe possible scores, not norms or percentiles. Mean of 2 responses, after reversing the items specified by the key. No verified clinical categories are assigned to this result here.","presentationDirection":"neutral","recommendedReview":false,"reliableDirection":true,"source":"https://gosling.psy.utexas.edu/scales-weve-developed/ten-item-personality-measure-tipi/ten-item-personality-inventory-tipi/","summary":"Higher scores describe more calm and emotional steadiness; lower scores describe more anxiety and becoming upset easily. The direction is opposite to a neuroticism score. Two items cannot diagnose anxiety or assess coping in every situation. This continuous self-description uses Holistic House explanatory prose, not a validated interpretation category; scale endpoints describe possible scores, not norms or percentiles.","verified":true},"values":[1,1.5,2,2.5,3,3.5,4,4.5,5,5.5,6,6.5,7]}]},{"allowedValues":[1,1.5,2,2.5,3,3.5,4,4.5,5,5.5,6,6.5,7],"mode":"descriptive","signature":{"dimensionClass":"trait","direction":"non-normative","key":"trait.tipi.openness","max":7,"min":1,"sourceConstruct":"Openness to experience","timeframe":"general-self-description","unit":"points"},"source":"https://gosling.psy.utexas.edu/scales-weve-developed/ten-item-personality-measure-tipi/ten-item-personality-inventory-tipi/","variants":[{"template":{"actions":["For {{value}} (1–7) on openness to experience, record a situation that fits this description and an exception. Explore flexibility rather than aiming for a “correct” score.","If useful: If you want variety, try a small reversible new experience; keeping a familiar preference is also a valid choice."],"bands":[],"basis":"descriptive","code":"continuous","construct":"Openness to experience","continuous":true,"currentBand":null,"label":"Descriptive score (1–7)","limitations":["No verified categorical cutoffs are applied to this version here. The score is not a diagnosis, norm or percentile."],"meaning":"Openness to experience: {{value}} (1–7). Higher scores describe more openness to unfamiliar experiences and a less conventional self-image; lower scores describe more conventional preferences. This two-item estimate is not an intelligence test or a ranking of lifestyles. This continuous self-description uses Holistic House explanatory prose, not a validated interpretation category; scale endpoints describe possible scores, not norms or percentiles. Mean of 2 responses, after reversing the items specified by the key. No verified clinical categories are a